<h2 id="for-experts">For Experts (or enthusiasts) <a class="heading-anchor" href="#for-experts" aria-label="Permalink to For Experts (or enthusiasts)">#</a></h2>

<p class="muted">How the personas are kept apart, how consult calls work, and what
the tests cover.</p>

<h3 id="shared-archive">Separate lives</h3>

<p>Each persona has its own repository, entry file, private memory and audit.
Neither imports the other's files. What they share is the Grimoire, reached
through a <code>lore/</code> link or clone.</p>

<p>That separation is on purpose. One persona's private notes and voice should
not leak into the other, and either one should be deployable on its own.</p>

<h3 id="asking-a-friend">The consult</h3>

<p>They can still work together. <code>series/agents.json</code> lists the agents
in the series. In the OpenCode deployment, the sync reads that list and installs
two things per persona: the main entry and a read-only consult copy. OpenClaw
uses the same list, but its consult entries are added in configuration.</p>

<p>One persona can ask a peer's consult copy a question. In OpenCode that is the
subagent tool; in OpenClaw it is <code>sessions_spawn</code> with the peer's
<code>-consult</code> id. The copy reads the Grimoire and answers in character,
but cannot edit files, run shell commands or ask someone else. In OpenCode it
also cannot read the peer's private folder. Its reply is lore to think about, not
instructions to follow.</p>

<h3 id="tests">The tests</h3>

<p>Each persona repository ships its own audit. The Drifter currently passes 20/20
suites and Eris 15/15. The suites cover voice, quotes, module completeness, the
conversation path and the shared structure gates.</p>

